The subject line on this commit is wrong. It says "fix the app look on
photobook A3 size"; nothing here touches layout or sizing. What it actually
adds is the order archive — GET /api/admin/studio-orders/:id/archive, which
hands the studio every original from a book as one zip.

One photo per click is forty clicks for a wedding. This returns the lot,
with design.json first, because otherwise the studio has a folder of
photographs and no idea which one belongs on which page.

Built by hand and streamed, because it has to be. A forty-photo book is
comfortably larger than the 128 MB a Worker gets, so nothing may be held in
memory: each object is piped straight from R2 into the response as it
arrives. That rules out knowing a file's size or CRC before its header is
written, which is what the zip data descriptor is for — the local header
sets the 0x08 flag to say "sizes follow the data", and they do. Stored, not
deflated; these are JPEGs and PNGs and compressing them again costs CPU to
save nothing.

An object missing from the bucket becomes an empty entry instead of aborting
the archive, so the studio gets everything that does exist and can see at a
glance which file did not.

The admin button that calls this arrived later, in ce4a922.
